#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718446 :question/problem "![](images/3a/3a8a91b00aadcac1ff2b5a22d49e2a9b1e4aa77337cf4c02603dc724867242ac.png)\n\nThe gray arrows show the non-constant field $\\mathbf F(x,y)=y\\mathbf i-x\\mathbf j$. Let $C$ be the upper unit semicircle traversed from $A=(-1,0)$ to $B=(1,0)$, and let $-C$ denote the same curve traversed from $B$ to $A$.\n\nWhich statements are true?\n\nI. $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$\n\nII. $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$\n\nIII. $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$" :question/worked-solution "The line integral is the total work done by the field along the oriented path. A force component along the motion contributes positive work; a component against the motion contributes negative work; a perpendicular force contributes zero work.\n\nFrom $A$ to $B$ along the upper semicircle, the particle travels clockwise. The force arrows are tangent to the circle and point clockwise, so the force assists the motion throughout the path.\n\n![](images/be/be22e2ebd35e15a5e7c05e7745e47380b4b9fb9020ebd7a6746413a64b1676fc.png)\n\nThus $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r>0$: statement I is true and statement II is false.\n\nOn reversing the path, the field stays fixed but the motion reverses, so the force now opposes the motion throughout.\n\n![](images/75/75830446bd7b22171226b50904233020686186552d6dbcc20d6b4d444a0468dd.png)\n\nTherefore $\\int_{-C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r<0$, so statement III is true.\n\nThe correct answer is **I and III only**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718452 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=- \\frac{1}{y} - \\frac{1}{x}$, where $x=e^{- t \\left(s - 1\\right)}$ and $y=e^{t \\left(s - 1\\right)}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to t](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along each path to $t$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{1}{x^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{1}{y^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the outer partial derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{2 t \\left(s - 1\\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{- 2 t \\left(s - 1\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, holding $s$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=\\left(1 - s\\right) e^{- t \\left(s - 1\\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=\\left(s - 1\\right) e^{t \\left(s - 1\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify the products and sum.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(e^{2 t \\left(s - 1\\right)}\\right)\\left(\\left(1 - s\\right) e^{- t \\left(s - 1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(e^{- 2 t \\left(s - 1\\right)}\\right)\\left(\\left(s - 1\\right) e^{t \\left(s - 1\\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(1 - s\\right) e^{t \\left(s - 1\\right)} + \\left(s - 1\\right) e^{- t \\left(s - 1\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718461 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=2 x^{2} - y^{2}$, where $x=s \\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}$ and $y=\\left(t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(s \\right)}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to t](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along each path to $t$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=4 x, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the outer partial derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=4 s \\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- 2 \\left(t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(s \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, holding $s$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=s \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=\\sin{\\left(s \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify the products and sum.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(4 s \\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}\\right)\\left(s \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(- 2 \\left(t + 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(s \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(s \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\left(2 s^{2} \\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)} - \\left(t + 1\\right) \\sin^{2}{\\left(s \\right)}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718476 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=e^{x + y}$, where $x=\\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$ and $y=\\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to t](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along each path to $t$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{x + y}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{x + y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the outer partial derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{\\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{\\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, holding $s$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=\\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=- \\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify the products and sum.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(e^{\\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right)\\left(\\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(e^{\\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\left(s - 1\\right) \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}}\\right)\\left(- \\left(s - 1\\right) \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(s - 1\\right) \\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) e^{\\left(s - 1\\right) \\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718479 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x y + 2 y$, where $x=\\left(t + 1\\right) e^{s}$ and $y=s e^{s}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to t](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along each path to $t$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the outer partial derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=s e^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\left(t + 1\\right) e^{s} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $t$, holding $s$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=e^{s}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify the products and sum.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(s e^{s}\\right)\\left(e^{s}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(\\left(t + 1\\right) e^{s} + 2\\right)\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=s e^{2 s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718481 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x - y$, where $x=- s \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}$ and $y=s \\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$." :question/worked-solution "![Chain-rule paths to s](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply along each path to $s$, then add the two products.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives, treating $x$ and $y$ as independent.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite the outer partial derivatives in terms of $s$ and $t$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext differentiate the intermediate variables with respect to $s$, holding $t$ fixed.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}&=- \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}, \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}&=\\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute into the chain rule and simplify the products and sum.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=\\left(1\\right)\\left(- \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &\\quad {}+\\left(-1\\right)\\left(\\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}\\right) \\\\ &=- (\\sin{\\left(t + 1 \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t + 1 \\right)}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718523 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=x \\ln{\\left(y^{2} + 1 \\right)} - 2 x - 1,\\\\Q&=\\frac{x^{2} y}{y^{2} + 1} - 2 y + 1,\\\\P_y&=\\frac{2 x y}{y^{2} + 1},\\\\Q_x&=\\frac{2 x y}{y^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 e^{2 x - 2 y} + 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 e^{2 x - 2 y},\\\\P_y&=- 4 e^{2 x - 2 y},\\\\Q_x&=- 4 e^{2 x - 2 y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- x \\ln{\\left(y^{2} + 1 \\right)} - x - 1,\\\\Q&=- \\frac{x^{2} y}{y^{2} + 1} - y + 1,\\\\P_y&=- \\frac{2 x y}{y^{2} + 1},\\\\Q_x&=- \\frac{2 x y}{y^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$, $\\mathbf G$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718531 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=\\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q&=\\cos{\\left(x + y \\right)} + 1,\\\\P_y&=- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=- \\sin{\\left(x + y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 x \\ln{\\left(y^{2} + 1 \\right)} - x,\\\\Q&=- \\frac{3 x^{2} y}{y^{2} + 1} - y - 1,\\\\P_y&=\\frac{4 x y}{y^{2} + 1},\\\\Q_x&=- \\frac{6 x y}{y^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $5\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 e^{x - 2 y} - 1,\\\\Q&=1 - 4 e^{x - 2 y},\\\\P_y&=- 4 e^{x - 2 y},\\\\Q_x&=- 4 e^{x - 2 y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718540 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 e^{x - y} - 1,\\\\Q&=- e^{x - y} - 1,\\\\P_y&=- 2 e^{x - y},\\\\Q_x&=- e^{x - y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-1\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y e^{2 x} + 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 y - e^{2 x},\\\\P_y&=- 2 e^{2 x},\\\\Q_x&=- 2 e^{2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + y \\right)} + 1,\\\\Q&=3 \\cos{\\left(2 x + y \\right)},\\\\P_y&=- 2 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=- 6 \\sin{\\left(2 x + y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(1,0)$, the difference is $4 \\sin{\\left(2 \\right)}\\ne0$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf G$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718544 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- x - 2 \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q&=- y,\\\\P_y&=- \\frac{2}{y^{2} + 1},\\\\Q_x&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-2\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=\\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q&=1 - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\\\P_y&=\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=\\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- x + \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)} + 1,\\\\Q&=\\frac{x}{y^{2} + 1} - y + 1,\\\\P_y&=\\frac{1}{y^{2} + 1},\\\\Q_x&=\\frac{1}{y^{2} + 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf G$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718550 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 4 x y - 2 x,\\\\Q&=- 2 x^{2} - 2 y,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x,\\\\Q_x&=- 4 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x y^{3} + 2 x - 1,\\\\Q&=- 9 x^{2} y^{2} + 2 y,\\\\P_y&=- 6 x y^{2},\\\\Q_x&=- 18 x y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $12\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y e^{2 x} + 1,\\\\Q&=y + e^{2 x},\\\\P_y&=- 2 e^{2 x},\\\\Q_x&=2 e^{2 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-4\\ne0$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718638 :question/problem "![Upper semicircle with diameter endpoints A(-1,1) and B(5,1)](images/de/dec59c468bd95a9e3e641209b688a1fa0c0cb6fa0d078730d87653820e2cce7e.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the upper semicircular arc with diameter $AB$, traversed once from $B$ to $A$. The dashed diameter is a guide, not part of the path." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of the diameter are $A(-1,1)$ and $B(5,1)$. Since they have the same $y$-coordinate,\n\n$$\nd=AB=5-(-1)=6,\\qquad r=\\frac{d}{2}=3.\n$$\n\nThe length of the semicircular arc is half the circumference:\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi r)=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nThe diameter is not traversed. Reversing direction would leave the arc length unchanged."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718641 :question/problem "![Clockwise circle with diagonal diameter from A(-2,-1) to B(2,3)](images/8e/8e7bb5ef8527a97d61481cd545dbbeb4a30ef383b8f8fa273aee9061d1472f2d.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the circle with diameter $AB$, traversed once clockwise. The dashed segment $AB$ is a guide, not part of the path." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nFirst find the diameter using the endpoint coordinates. The right triangle below is an auxiliary construction, not an additional path:\n\n![Right triangle with horizontal and vertical legs of length 4 along diameter AB](images/df/df4ccec99dcb2e5bc87533203c10475d5052c1dbc46ee699c4ce5f7d3695e7fb.png)\n\nThe coordinate differences are $2-(-2)=4$ and $3-(-1)=4$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nd=AB&=\\sqrt{(2-(-2))^2+(3-(-1))^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4^2+4^2}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA full traversal has length equal to the circumference:\n\n$$\nL=\\pi d=4\\pi\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\oint_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=4\\pi\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nA closed path has zero net displacement, but its arc length is positive. Clockwise traversal does not make an integral with respect to $s$ negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718644 :question/problem "![Semicircle of radius 2 with counterclockwise arrows](images/24/244e830a32373fc20379f9b55650452c77e1a422ce5ee9456007cdac0d0daebc.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the semicircular arc of radius $2$ shown above, traversed once counterclockwise." :question/worked-solution "The line integral of unity with respect to arc length equals the length $L$ of the path:\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nHere $C$ is half of a circle of radius $2$, so\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi r)=\\pi(2)=2\\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=2\\pi.\n$$\n\nOnly the curved arc is traversed; the radius drawn as a dashed guide is not part of $C$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718663 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 y e^{2 x y} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} + 1,\\\\Q&=x e^{2 x y} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y e^{2 x y} - 2 e^{2 x y} + \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=2 x y e^{2 x y} + e^{2 x y} - 3 \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-3\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=4 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - y + 1,\\\\Q&=- 6 x^{2} y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - x + 1,\\\\P_y&=8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q_x&=- 12 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $20 \\cos{\\left(1 \\right)}\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y e^{x y} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q&=3 x e^{x y} + 3 \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\P_y&=x y e^{x y} + e^{x y} + \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=3 x y e^{x y} + 3 e^{x y} - 3 \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-2\\ne0$.\n\nSelect **None of the above**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718666 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 2 y + 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 x^{2} y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 2 x,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=- 4 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 2 y - 1,\\\\Q&=- x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + x,\\\\P_y&=x y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=x y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $1\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- y \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 2 y,\\\\Q&=2 x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} - 1,\\\\P_y&=x y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-1\\ne0$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718681 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 y,\\\\Q&=x \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 x + 1,\\\\P_y&=- 2 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 2 y,\\\\Q&=- 2 x - 1,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 2,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $- 4 \\cos{\\left(1 \\right)}\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 4 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - y - 1,\\\\Q&=- 4 x^{2} y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - x,\\\\P_y&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q_x&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718690 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 4 x \\sin{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + y - 1,\\\\Q&=- 4 x^{2} y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + x,\\\\P_y&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 1,\\\\Q_x&=- 8 x y \\cos{\\left(y^{2} \\right)} + 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 y \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 y + 1,\\\\Q&=2 x \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 x,\\\\P_y&=- 4 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2,\\\\Q_x&=- 4 x y \\sin{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x y \\right)} + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=y e^{2 x y} + \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\Q&=x e^{2 x y} - \\cos{\\left(x - y \\right)} - 1,\\\\P_y&=2 x y e^{2 x y} + e^{2 x y} + \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)},\\\\Q_x&=2 x y e^{2 x y} + e^{2 x y} + \\sin{\\left(x - y \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$, $\\mathbf G$, $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718717 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nFor $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ with continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, the field is conservative exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere. The partial derivatives are supplied:\n\n| Field | $P_y$ | $Q_x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\mathbf F$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |\n| $\\mathbf G$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $\\mathbf H$ | $8 x y$ | $- 12 x y$ |" :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=x - y - 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 x + y - 1,\\\\P_y&=-1,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $1\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x - 2 y - 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 x - 2 y,\\\\P_y&=-2,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf G$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=4 x y^{2} + 2 x + 1,\\\\Q&=- 6 x^{2} y + 2 y,\\\\P_y&=8 x y,\\\\Q_x&=- 12 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $20\\ne0$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf G$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718718 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nFor $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ with continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, the field is conservative exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere. The partial derivatives are supplied:\n\n| Field | $P_y$ | $Q_x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\mathbf F$ | $- 12 x y^{2}$ | $- 18 x y^{2}$ |\n| $\\mathbf G$ | $2 x$ | $- 2 x$ |\n| $\\mathbf H$ | $12 x y^{2}$ | $12 x y^{2}$ |" :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 4 x y^{3} + 2 x - 1,\\\\Q&=- 9 x^{2} y^{2} + 2 y,\\\\P_y&=- 12 x y^{2},\\\\Q_x&=- 18 x y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $6\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 x y + 2 x,\\\\Q&=- x^{2} + 2 y - 1,\\\\P_y&=2 x,\\\\Q_x&=- 2 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(1,0)$, the difference is $4\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=4 x y^{3} - 2 x,\\\\Q&=6 x^{2} y^{2} - 2 y,\\\\P_y&=12 x y^{2},\\\\Q_x&=12 x y^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf H$ is conservative.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf H$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718739 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nFor $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ with continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, the field is conservative exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere. The partial derivatives are supplied:\n\n| Field | $P_y$ | $Q_x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\mathbf F$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n| $\\mathbf G$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| $\\mathbf H$ | $8 x y$ | $- 8 x y$ |" :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=x - y,\\\\Q&=3 x + y,\\\\P_y&=-1,\\\\Q_x&=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf F$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-4\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x + y,\\\\Q&=2 x - 2 y - 1,\\\\P_y&=1,\\\\Q_x&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-1\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=4 x y^{2} - x - 1,\\\\Q&=- 4 x^{2} y - y - 1,\\\\P_y&=8 x y,\\\\Q_x&=- 8 x y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(1,1)$, the difference is $16\\ne0$.\n\nSelect **None of the above**."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718750 :question/problem "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^2$? Select all that apply.\n\nFor $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ with continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$, the field is conservative exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere. The partial derivatives are supplied:\n\n| Field | $P_y$ | $Q_x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\mathbf F$ | $-2$ | $-2$ |\n| $\\mathbf G$ | $1$ | $-2$ |\n| $\\mathbf H$ | $0$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "All components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. Polynomial, exponential, trigonometric and arctangent functions are smooth here; any logarithm has positive argument and any denominator is strictly positive. Thus $\\mathbf V=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$ is **conservative** exactly when $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nFor $\\mathbf F$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=x - 2 y + 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 x + y + 1,\\\\P_y&=-2,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$P_y=Q_x$ is an identity, so $\\mathbf F$ is conservative.\n\nFor $\\mathbf G$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=- 2 x + y + 1,\\\\Q&=- 2 x - 2 y - 1,\\\\P_y&=1,\\\\Q_x&=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf G$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $3\\ne0$.\n\nFor $\\mathbf H$, differentiate each component with respect to the indicated variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P&=2 x - 1,\\\\Q&=2 x + 2 y,\\\\P_y&=0,\\\\Q_x&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivatives are not equal everywhere, so $\\mathbf H$ is not conservative. At $(0,0)$, the difference is $-2\\ne0$.\n\nSelect $\\mathbf F$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718764 :question/problem "![](images/be/bebc358e1c941a03af63acc51e653bfda72ca5d5e4519a325b20c6c8341cc240.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the closed path shown, traversed once in the direction of the arrows. The curved part from $B$ to $A$ is a semicircle with diameter $AB$; the other two parts are straight segments." :question/worked-solution "The three pieces are the semicircular arc $C_1$, the segment $AD$ labeled $C_2$, and the segment $DB$ labeled $C_3$:\n\n![](images/54/541c8d8ad7b5905709270e8774e874fc70063984cf6be9014cd158095e36c848.png)\n\nThe line integral of unity equals the total path length, so\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2+L_3.\n$$\n\nThe diameter has endpoints $A(-2,1)$ and $B(4,1)$. Its length is $4-(-2)=6$, so the radius is $3$ and\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac12(2\\pi\\cdot3)=3\\pi.\n$$\n\nUse the distance formula separately for the two straight pieces. With $D(0,-3)$,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_2&=\\sqrt{(0-(-2))^2+(-3-1)^2}=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5},\\\\\nL_3&=\\sqrt{(4-0)^2+(1-(-3))^2}=\\sqrt{32}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=3\\pi+2\\sqrt{5}+4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThe diameter drawn with a dashed line is a construction aid, not part of $C$. Each of the three path lengths contributes positively."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718772 :question/problem "![](images/ef/efd46d59336f6f2039e89f58cd330158324f255264d48bb943dfbaf40fd8239f.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ follows the upper semicircle of radius $3$ and then its diameter, once in the direction of the arrows." :question/worked-solution "The path has two pieces, as shown below:\n\n![](images/eb/eb33864bcf8b11f8393dbf2425519f329667e39fbf8eb1b61bfd4543b3d143ff.png)\n\nThe line integral of unity equals the path's length. Additivity gives\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_1}\\,\\mathrm{d}s+\\int_{C_2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nThe semicircular arc $C_1$ has length $\\frac12(2\\pi\\cdot3)=3\\pi$. The diameter $C_2$ has length $2\\cdot3=6$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=3\\pi+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718777 :question/problem "![](images/c8/c824023213be3c003f60fd409b7812d43d21634ccbd1679586d4d76b191fe3e7.png)\n\nCalculate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s,\n$$\nwhere $C$ follows the quarter-circle arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin, then the straight chord joining its endpoints, once in the direction of the arrows." :question/worked-solution "Split the path into the quarter-circle arc $C_1$ and the chord $C_2$:\n\n![](images/dc/dc02578579c30837a8f7041f87fa339fe708602f767f9cfc565b58a93cbb1d40.png)\n\nSince $\\int_C\\,\\mathrm{d}s$ is the total length, add the two lengths. The quarter-circle arc has length\n\n$$\nL_1=\\frac14(2\\pi\\cdot3)=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe chord joins $(0,3)$ to $(3,0)$. By the distance formula,\n\n$$\nL_2=\\sqrt{(3-0)^2+(0-3)^2}=\\sqrt{18}=3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\,\\mathrm{d}s=L_1+L_2=\\frac{3\\pi}{2}+3\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718784 :question/problem "Consider the curves\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nC_1:\\quad \\mathbf r_1(t)&=\\langle t,t^2\\rangle, &0\\le t\\le 2,\\\\\nC_2:\\quad \\mathbf r_2(u)&=\\langle 2u,4u^2\\rangle, &0\\le u\\le 1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose $f$ is continuous along the curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-3.\n$$\n\nThe notation $-C_1$ means $C_1$ with its orientation reversed. Which statements must be true? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For $C_1$, eliminating $t$ gives $y=x^2$ with $0\\le x\\le 2$. For $C_2$, $x=2u$ gives $y=4u^2=x^2$, again with $0\\le x\\le 2$. Both trace this entire parabolic arc once, in the same direction; equivalently, $\\mathbf r_2(u)=\\mathbf r_1(2u)$.\n\nIndependence of parametrization gives\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-3.\n$$\n\nChanging parameter speed does not multiply this value by $2$, so the proposed value $-6$ is incorrect. Independence of orientation also gives\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-3,\n$$\n\nso the proposed value $3$ is incorrect. Select only the statement with $\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=-3$. A scalar line integral may be negative when $f$ takes negative values; the nonnegative arc-length element does not force the integral to be positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718788 :question/problem "The diagram shows a curve $C$ from $A$ to $B$ and the same curve $-C$ traversed from $B$ to $A$. Each path traverses the curve once.\n\n![The same curve with opposite orientations: C from A to B and -C from B to A.](images/e4/e4f68e10429fa4bda69366751c303216d27df995edf2502b61ab9bfe84c3ff45.png)\n\nSuppose $f$ is continuous along the curve and\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=6.\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s$?" :question/worked-solution "A scalar line integral with respect to arc length is independent of orientation. Reversing the arrows does not change the arc-length element $\\mathrm{d}s$ or the values of $f$ along the curve. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=6.\n$$\n\nThe answer is $6$. Reversing orientation does not negate the integral, cancel it, or count the curve twice."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718798 :question/problem "The curve $C_1$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_1(t)=\\langle\\sqrt{1+9t^2},3t\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le t\\le \\frac13$. The curve $C_2$ is parametrized by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(u)=\\langle\\sqrt{1+(1-2u)^2},1-2u\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $0\\le u\\le \\frac12$.\n\nSuppose $f$ is continuous along the curves and\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nThe notation $-C_2$ means $C_2$ with its orientation reversed. Which statements must be true for every such $f$? Select all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For $C_1$, $y=3t$, so $x=\\sqrt{1+y^2}$ and $y$ increases from $0$ to $1$. For $C_2$, $y=1-2u$, so $x=\\sqrt{1+y^2}$ and $y$ decreases from $1$ to $0$. Thus both trace exactly once the same branch and interval,\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{1+y^2},\\qquad 0\\le y\\le 1,\n$$\n\nwith opposite orientations. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_2(u)=\\mathbf r_1\\!\\left(\\frac{1-2u}{3}\\right).\n$$\n\nA scalar line integral with respect to arc length is independent of both parametrization and orientation. Hence\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=\\int_{C_1} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nReversing $C_2$ once more also preserves the value:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-C_2} f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s=k.\n$$\n\nThe parameter change has derivative $-2/3$, but this is not a factor multiplying the line integral: the speed factor in $\\mathrm{d}s$ and the parameter interval change together. The proposed value $2k/3$ is therefore not guaranteed. It agrees with $k$ when $k=0$, but fails, for example, when $f=1$ and $k$ is the positive length of this arc.\n\nSelect the statements with values $k$ on $C_2$ and on $-C_2$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718821 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=e^{x + y + z}$, where $x=- s + 2 t$, $y=s^{2}$, $z=- s + t$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply along the three paths and add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{x + y + z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite these partials in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each intermediate variable with respect to $s$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}&=-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}&=2 s \\\\ \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the matching factors, then collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=\\left(e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t}\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t}\\right)\\left(2 s\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t}\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=2 \\left(s - 1\\right) e^{s^{2} - 2 s + 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718822 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=3 x + y - 2 z$, where $x=3 s^{2} + t$, $y=s^{2} + 4 t^{2}$, $z=- s + 2 t^{2}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along the three paths and add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite these partials in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=3 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each intermediate variable with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=8 t \\\\ \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}&=4 t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the matching factors, then collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(3\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(1\\right)\\left(8 t\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(-2\\right)\\left(4 t\\right) \\\\ &=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718824 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=e^{x + y + z}$, where $x=s + 2 t$, $y=- s + t$, $z=- s^{2}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply along the three paths and add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{x + y + z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite these partials in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{- s^{2} + 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{- s^{2} + 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{- s^{2} + 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each intermediate variable with respect to $s$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}&=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}&=-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}&=- 2 s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the matching factors, then collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=\\left(e^{- s^{2} + 3 t}\\right)\\left(1\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{- s^{2} + 3 t}\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{- s^{2} + 3 t}\\right)\\left(- 2 s\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 s e^{- s^{2} + 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718830 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=e^{x + y + z}$, where $x=- 2 s + 2 t$, $y=- 2 t^{2}$, $z=s - t$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nHold $s$ fixed. Multiply along the three paths and add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{x + y + z} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{x + y + z}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite these partials in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each intermediate variable with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t}&=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}&=- 4 t \\\\ \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}&=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the matching factors, then collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}&=\\left(e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t}\\right)\\left(2\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t}\\right)\\left(- 4 t\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t}\\right)\\left(-1\\right) \\\\ &=- \\left(4 t - 1\\right) e^{- s - 2 t^{2} + t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718833 :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)=x y z$, where $x=s e^{- 3 t}$, $y=-2$, $z=t e^{- 2 s}$. Calculate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}$ in terms of $s$ and $t$." :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nHold $t$ fixed. Multiply along the three paths and add:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}\n$$\n\nFirst find the outer partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=y z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=x z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWrite these partials in terms of $s$ and $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- 2 t e^{- 2 s} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=s t e^{- 2 s - 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=- 2 s e^{- 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each intermediate variable with respect to $s$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s}&=e^{- 3 t} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}&=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}&=- 2 t e^{- 2 s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombine exponential factors using $e^A e^B=e^{A+B}$ when collecting the path products. Substitute the matching factors, then collect terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial s}&=\\left(- 2 t e^{- 2 s}\\right)\\left(e^{- 3 t}\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(s t e^{- 2 s - 3 t}\\right)\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &{}+\\left(- 2 s e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\left(- 2 t e^{- 2 s}\\right) \\\\ &=2 t \\left(2 s - 1\\right) e^{- 2 s - 3 t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718845 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sin{(x )} \\cos{(y )}$, $x=- t^{2}$ and $y=- t$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}$ in terms of $t$." :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to t](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nMultiply along each path and add the contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\cos{(x )} \\cos{(y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\sin{(x )} \\sin{(y )}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the negative arguments, use $\\sin(-u)=-\\sin(u)$ and $\\cos(-u)=\\cos(u)$.\n\nSubstitute both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\cos{((- t^{2}) )} \\cos{((- t) )} \\\\ &=\\cos{(t )} \\cos{(t^{2} )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\sin{((- t^{2}) )} \\sin{((- t) )} \\\\ &=- \\sin{(t )} \\sin{(t^{2} )}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=- 2 t \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute each path product, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=(\\cos{(t )} \\cos{(t^{2} )})(- 2 t) \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=(- \\sin{(t )} \\sin{(t^{2} )})(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}t}&=\\begin{aligned}&- 2 t \\cos{(t )} \\cos{(t^{2} )}\\\\&{}+(\\sin{(t )} \\sin{(t^{2} )})\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718851 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y + 1}$, $x=s^{2}$ and $y=s$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}$ in terms of $s$. Assume $s + 1\\ne0$." :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nMultiply along each path and add the contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{1}{y + 1} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{x}{(y + 1)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{1}{s + 1} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- \\frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=2 s \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute each path product, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\left(\\frac{1}{s + 1}\\right)(2 s) \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\left(- \\frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}\\right)(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\begin{aligned}&\\frac{2 s}{s + 1}\\\\&{}+\\left(- \\frac{s^{2}}{(s + 1)^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718854 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x \\cos{(y )}$, $x=- 3 s$ and $y=- s$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}$ in terms of $s$." :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nMultiply along each path and add the contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\cos{(y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- x \\sin{(y )}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the negative arguments, use $\\sin(-u)=-\\sin(u)$ and $\\cos(-u)=\\cos(u)$.\n\nSubstitute both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\cos{((- s) )} \\\\ &=\\cos{(s )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- (- 3 s) \\sin{((- s) )} \\\\ &=- 3 s \\sin{(s )}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=-3 \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute each path product, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=(\\cos{(s )})(-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=(- 3 s \\sin{(s )})(-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\begin{aligned}&- 3 \\cos{(s )}\\\\&{}+(3 s \\sin{(s )})\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718865 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=x \\ln{(y )} + y$, $x=2 s$ and $y=2 s$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}$ in terms of $s$. Assume $s>0$." :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nMultiply along each path and add the contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\ln{(y )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{x}{y} + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\ln{(2 s )} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute each path product, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=(\\ln{(2 s )})(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=(2)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\begin{aligned}&2 \\ln{(2 s )}\\\\&{}+(4)\\end{aligned}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718875 :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{2xy}$, $x=2 s$ and $y=\\ln{(2 s )}$, compute $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}$ in terms of $s$. Assume $s>0$ and $2 s>1$." :question/worked-solution "![Two chain-rule paths through x and y to s](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nMultiply along each path and add the contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the partial derivatives, holding the other intermediate variable constant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{2xy}} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2xy}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=\\frac{\\ln{(2 s )}}{\\sqrt{2(2 s)(\\ln{(2 s )})}} \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=\\frac{2 s}{\\sqrt{2(2 s)(\\ln{(2 s )})}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate the coordinate functions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=2 \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{1}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCompute each path product, then add.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\left(\\frac{\\ln{(2 s )}}{\\sqrt{2(2 s)(\\ln{(2 s )})}}\\right)(2) \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\left(\\frac{2 s}{\\sqrt{2(2 s)(\\ln{(2 s )})}}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{s}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{\\mathrm{d}f}{\\mathrm{d}s}&=\\frac{2 \\ln{(2 s )} + (2)}{\\sqrt{2(2 s)(\\ln{(2 s )})}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718902 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(a x y^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(2 x^{2} y + 3\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=2 a x y \\\\ Q_x&=4 x y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality must hold for every point of the plane. Simplifying this identity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a - 2&=0 \\\\ a&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe matching terms cancel as identities. Any common factor that vanishes at some points is nonzero elsewhere; equality at those zeros alone would not determine the constant.\n\nAt $a=2$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186718908 :question/problem "Consider the vector field\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf F&=\\bigl(y e^{y + x} - 2 x^{2}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf i \\\\ &{}+\\bigl(- 2 y^{2} + \\left(- 2 a + y\\right) e^{y + x}\\bigr)\\,\\mathbf j\\end{aligned}\n$$\non $\\mathbb R^2$, where $a$ is a constant. Given that $\\mathbf F$ is conservative, find $a$." :question/worked-solution "Write $\\mathbf F=P\\,\\mathbf i+Q\\,\\mathbf j$. Both components have continuous first partial derivatives on $\\mathbb R^2$. On this domain, the conservative-field test is $P_y=Q_x$ everywhere.\n\nThe required partial derivatives are\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P_y&=y e^{y + x} + e^{y + x} \\\\ Q_x&=\\left(- 2 a + y\\right) e^{y + x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquality must hold for every point of the plane. Simplifying this identity gives\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 a + 1&=0 \\\\ a&=- \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe matching terms cancel as identities. Any common factor that vanishes at some points is nonzero elsewhere; equality at those zeros alone would not determine the constant.\n\nAt $a=- \\frac{1}{2}$, the two displayed partial derivatives agree everywhere, so this value satisfies the conservative-field condition."}]}
